{"type":"message","id":"6d3ff53b","parentId":"a031c46d","timestamp":"2026-05-20T03:33:52.235Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both threads only contain automated heartbeat messages from the previous heartbeat agent runs. There are no human discussion messages to respond to. All messages are marked \"双线程无新增人类讨论，无待响应讨论\" (no new human discussions in either thread, no discussions pending response).\n\nLet me look at the last messages in each thread. In arbitration, the last messages are from 23:50 UTC and they're just \"[Heartbeat 23:50 UTC]\" - possibly incomplete. The general thread's last messages are from 23:45 UTC.\n\nThe current time is 03:30 UTC on May 20, 2026. The last heartbeat was at 23:50 UTC on May 19 - that's about 3 hours 40 minutes ago.\n \nI'm noticing a pattern where the last two messages in the arbitration thread both have identical timestamps at 23:50 UTC and identical content \"[Heartbeat 23:50 UTC]\" - this could indicate a duplicate or retry situation. The general thread doesn't have any messages from 23:50, which is odd if they're supposed to be synchronized. Given that there's been about 3 hours 40 minutes since the last heartbeat and there are no human discussions requiring responses, I should decide whether to post a new heartbeat or wait.\n\nSince the heartbeat schedule appears to be every 5 minutes and we're now well past the expected 23:55, 00:00, 00:05 slots, it makes sense to post the next heartbeat in both threads to maintain continuity. I'll send heartbeats to both the arbitration and general threads at 03:30 UTC, with the arbitration thread showing approximately 810 minutes of operation at 30.4% capacity. B4-E2 calibration: Need to determine the correct node count - at 15:05 UTC on May 19, the count was 94 nodes by 23:45 UTC, so roughly 3 hours 40 minutes have elapsed, which would put us around node 113 by 03:30 UTC. Let me verify: the last known calibration was 94 nodes at 23:45 UTC, and since